---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CommercialContinuousScco2SmeModeler from '../../components/CommercialContinuousScco2SmeModeler.astro';

const pageTitle = "Commercial Continuous Supercritical CO2 Static-Mixer Micro-Encapsulation & Core-Shell (scCO2-SME) Modeler | IsItKitchenSafe";
const pageDescription = "Simulate continuous dense-phase supercritical carbon dioxide static-mixer micro-encapsulation (scCO2-SME, 100–320 bar, 35–65°C) for solvent-free, non-thermal sub-micron core-shell lipid and protein encapsulation of live probiotics, hydrophobic vitamins, and delicate terpenes under FDA 21 CFR § 184.1240 GRAS, USP <467>, ASME BPVC Section VIII, and 3-A Sanitary Standards 60-01 & 61-01.";

const breadcrumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: 'Tools', href: '/tools/' },
  { label: 'Commercial Continuous scCO2-SME Encapsulation Modeler' }
];

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "How does continuous supercritical CO2 static-mixer micro-encapsulation (scCO2-SME) encapsulate live probiotics and sensitive bioactives without heat or toxic solvents?",
    answer: "Traditional micro-encapsulation methods rely on either high-temperature spray drying (140–190°C air), which causes thermal inactivation of live probiotic strains (e.g., Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium) and volatile loss of terpenes, or organic solvent evaporation/coacervation (using dichloromethane or acetone), which generates cytotoxic volatile residues prohibited in clean-label food products. Continuous scCO2-SME resolves these issues by using carbon dioxide above its critical point (Tc = 31.1°C, Pc = 73.8 bar) as a green, non-toxic fluidizing and atomizing agent at physiological temperatures (35–40°C). Supercritical CO2 dissolves rapidly into liquid lipids and plasticizes biopolymer shells (whey protein isolate, sodium alginate, lecithin, shellac) at 100–250 bar. As the stream passes through high-intensity static mixer elements (such as Sulzer SMX Plus or Kenics KM), it experiences micro-vortices and uniform shear rates up to 45,000 s^-1, dispersing the active core droplets into a homogeneous sub-micron emulsion. Upon continuous decompression through an induction-heated micro-nozzle into an aseptic collection chamber, the sudden pressure drop triggers instantaneous CO2 flashing from dense fluid to gas. This desolvation induces rapid phase separation and crystallization of the shell polymer, locking the core payload securely inside solid, monodisperse microcapsules (Sauter mean diameter 180–450 nm) with encapsulation efficiencies exceeding 97.4% and 0.0 ppm solvent residues."
  },
  {
    question: "What physical mechanisms govern the RESS/PGSS expansion and droplet formation kinetics in dense-phase CO2 micro-encapsulation?",
    answer: "The process couples two thermodynamic mechanisms: Rapid Expansion of Supercritical Solutions (RESS) and Particles from Gas-Saturated Solutions (PGSS). When dense-phase CO2 dissolves into the carrier biopolymer at 120–250 bar, it reduces the carrier melting temperature and depresses dynamic viscosity by up to 90% while reducing interfacial tension by orders of magnitude according to the scaling relation gamma(P) = gamma_0 * exp(-k_gamma * P). In the static mixer, fluid lamellae are repeatedly divided into 2^n striations (where n is the number of elements), yielding striation thicknesses below 0.15 um. Upon continuous discharge through a laser-drilled coaxial micro-orifice (120–180 um) at decompression rates exceeding 10^7 bar/s, supercritical CO2 undergoes supersonic volumetric expansion (Mach 1.2–1.5). The sudden gas flashing tears the plasticized fluid filaments into monodisperse micro-droplets governed by Kolmogorov-Hinze turbulent breakup theory (d_32 proportional to (sigma / rho_c)^3/5 * epsilon^-2/5). The instantaneous gas expansion also removes latent heat and CO2 solvent molecules, precipitating a rigid solid shell around the dispersed core within microseconds."
  },
  {
    question: "How does coaxial induction nozzle heating counteract Joule-Thomson freeze-choking during high-pressure CO2 decompression?",
    answer: "When supercritical CO2 decompresses from high operating pressures (100–320 bar) down to ambient receiver pressure (1 bar), it undergoes massive isenthalpic expansion governed by the Joule-Thomson effect (Delta T_JT = integral mu_JT dP). For carbon dioxide, the Joule-Thomson coefficient mu_JT is strongly positive (approximately 1.1 to 1.6 K/bar at dense-phase conditions), resulting in an uncompensated adiabatic temperature drop of 40 to 75°C. In unheated capillary tubes, this extreme freezing immediately drops local fluid temperature below -56.6°C (the CO2 triple point), causing instantaneous desublimation of carbon dioxide into solid dry ice snow. Dry ice crystals pack against the orifice walls, choking flow, triggering severe upstream pressure spikes over 350 bar, and halting production. The commercial scCO2-SME system incorporates a coaxial high-frequency induction heating collar around the sapphire nozzle tip. Regulated by a fast closed-loop PID controller, this collar supplies continuous localized thermal energy to maintain the inner nozzle wall at +45 to +55°C, ensuring that expanding gas remains strictly in the gaseous vapor regime and guaranteeing 100% clog-free, steady-state continuous atomization."
  },
  {
    question: "Why is continuous scCO2-SME up to 78%+ more energy efficient than conventional industrial spray drying?",
    answer: "Industrial thermal spray dryers require vast amounts of thermal energy to vaporize water or solvents from liquid feeds—typically consuming between 1.8 and 2.6 kWh of thermal energy per kilogram of evaporated water, with thermal efficiencies rarely exceeding 45% due to massive hot exhaust air venting. In contrast, the continuous scCO2-SME process operates as a solvent-free mechanical precipitation system: because supercritical CO2 acts directly as the volatile solvent/plasticizer, no latent heat of water evaporation is required to precipitate the microcapsules. The mechanical power is consumed primarily by high-efficiency multi-stage positive displacement pumps compressing liquid CO2 to dense-phase pressures and operating sanitary static mixer contactors, requiring only 0.24 to 0.35 kWh/kg of encapsulated powder. Furthermore, over 95% of the decompressed gaseous CO2 is continuously reclaimed through the downstream aseptic cyclone separator, re-condensed in a closed refrigeration loop at 55 bar / -18°C, and recycled back into the high-pressure feed pump, slashing net operational energy demand by 78.5% compared to thermal spray dryers."
  },
  {
    question: "What regulatory compliance standards (FDA GRAS 21 CFR § 184.1240, USP <467>, ASME BPVC Section VIII, 3-A 60-01) govern scCO2-SME operations?",
    answer: "Commercial scCO2-SME skids operate under comprehensive food, pharmaceutical, and pressure safety frameworks. Under FDA 21 CFR § 184.1240, high-purity food-grade carbon dioxide (purity >= 99.95%) is affirmed as Generally Recognized as Safe (GRAS) for direct food contact and extraction. Under USP <467> and European Pharmacopoeia (Ph. Eur. 5.4) guidelines for residual solvents, supercritical CO2 processing is categorized as a Class 3 / Non-Solvent technology, producing active microcapsules with strictly 0.0 ppm volatile organic compounds (VOCs). Under ASME Boiler and Pressure Vessel Code (BPVC) Section VIII Divisions 1 and 2, all dense-phase contactors, static mixer housings, and high-pressure fluid piping are designed with 4:1 safety factors against cyclic fatigue at pressures up to 350 bar, protected by ASME UV-stamped safety pressure relief valves and burst discs. Under 3-A Sanitary Standards 60-01 & 61-01 and EHEDG Guidelines (Doc 8 & Doc 16), all product-wetted metallic components are constructed from electropolished AISI 316L stainless steel or Hastelloy C-22 (surface roughness Ra <= 0.38 um) with FDA-compliant perfluoroelastomer seals (Kalrez 6230), self-draining geometry, and full compatibility with automated Clean-in-Place (CIP) and Steam-in-Place (SIP at 121°C) sterilization."
  }
];

const structuredData = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "WebApplication",
      "@id": "https://isitkitchensafe.com/tools/commercial-continuous-scco2-sme-micro-encapsulation-modeler/#webapp",
      "url": "https://isitkitchensafe.com/tools/commercial-continuous-scco2-sme-micro-encapsulation-modeler/",
      "name": "Commercial Continuous Supercritical CO2 Static-Mixer Micro-Encapsulation & Core-Shell (scCO2-SME) Modeler",
      "applicationCategory": "EngineeringApplication",
      "operatingSystem": "All",
      "browserRequirements": "Requires JavaScript. Requires HTML5 Canvas/CSS3.",
      "offers": {
        "@type": "Offer",
        "price": "0",
        "priceCurrency": "USD"
      },
      "description": pageDescription
    },
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      "itemListElement": [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 1,
          "name": "Home",
          "item": "https://isitkitchensafe.com/"
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 2,
          "name": "Tools",
          "item": "https://isitkitchensafe.com/tools/"
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 3,
          "name": "Commercial Continuous scCO2-SME Modeler",
          "item": "https://isitkitchensafe.com/tools/commercial-continuous-scco2-sme-micro-encapsulation-modeler/"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": faqs.map(faq => ({
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": faq.question,
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": faq.answer
        }
      }))
    }
  ]
};
---

<Layout 
  title={pageTitle}
  description={pageDescription}
  structuredData={structuredData}
>
  <main class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-8">
    <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs} />

    <CommercialContinuousScco2SmeModeler lang="en" />

    <!-- Frequently Asked Questions -->
    <section class="mt-12 bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-3xl p-6 md:p-8">
      <h2 class="text-title-lg font-medium text-ink tracking-tight mb-2">
        Frequently Asked Questions: Continuous Supercritical CO2 Static-Mixer Encapsulation (scCO2-SME)
      </h2>
      <p class="text-body text-xs text-muted mb-6">
        Scientific, thermodynamic, and regulatory engineering answers regarding dense-phase micro-encapsulation, RESS/PGSS kinetics, and solvent-free clean-label biopolymer shell formation.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        {faqs.map((faq) => (
          <div class="border-b border-hairline/60 pb-5 last:border-0 last:pb-0">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-ink mb-2">
              {faq.question}
            </h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed text-muted">
              {faq.answer}
            </p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>
